course: dictionary
lesson: 1177
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can find the right one by reading the context, which means the words and sentences around the word. «Many birds that eat seeds feed their chicks insects for protein, and a pair of blue tits can bring around 10 000 caterpillars to one brood.» The chicks, the insects and the nest are clues. Here, a brood is the group of baby birds born together.
explain: Brood can also be a verb, a doing word. A hen broods her eggs, which means she sits on them to keep them warm until they hatch. If the sentence is about eggs, chicks or nests, brood is about birds.
explain: Stories use brood differently. «He sat brooding by the window, gloomy and silent.» Here brooding means thinking again and again about something that makes you sad or angry. Ask yourself: is this about animals, or about a person's feelings? The clues tell you which meaning to use.
explain: A good check is to swap in a simpler meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense. For a family of baby birds, try «a family of young animals». For the story, try «thinking sadly for a long time». If it fits, you have found the right meaning.
fact: «Brood» means a family of young animals born at the same time.
fact: «Brood» can also mean to sit on eggs to keep them warm until they hatch.
fact: «Brooding» means keeping on thinking about something that makes you unhappy or angry, in a gloomy way.
fact: In a humorous way, «brood» can mean a large family of children.
fact: A «broody» hen wants to sit on eggs. In informal UK English, a broody person wants a baby.
exercise: «A pair of blue tits can bring around 10 000 caterpillars to one brood.» What is one brood? | A tree full of green leaves | A hungry fox looking for food | A special kind of egg-shaped nest | *A family of baby birds born at the same time
exercise: In science, a hen broods her eggs. What does she do? | Paints them bright colours | Eats them for breakfast | *Sits on them to keep them warm until they hatch | Hides them under the river bank
exercise: «He sat brooding by the window, gloomy and silent.» What does brooding mean here? | Singing loudly and happily | *Keeping on thinking about something sad, in a gloomy way | Sleeping very deeply | Running fast around the garden
test: Which word means a family of young animals born at the same time? | cage | tide | shell | *brood
test: Which word is used humorously for a large family of children? | *brood | herd | pride | swarm
exam: In «She brooded over the lost match, silent and gloomy for hours», what does brooded mean? | Ran outside to search for the match | *Thought sadly and gloomily about something for a long time | Sat on a pile of eggs | Cooked a big dinner
